WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:11.000
[Dr. Urban:] Primary treatment of breast cancer succeeds when the tumor and its regional lymph-node metastasis are destroyed

00:01:11.010 --> 00:01:15.300
or removed completely in the absence of systemic spread.

00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:21.480
Appropriate surgery for specific clinical settings should be based, we believe, on gross pathological

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:28.200
and anatomical extent of disease present, and should aim to achieve optimal local control while 

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:32.520
interfering least with physical appearance and function of the patient.

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:36.200
Many factors are involved in this problem. 

00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:45.520
Perhaps we may start off best with the survey of the salvage of untreated breast cancer. 

00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:47.850
Let me have the first slide please.

00:01:47.850 --> 00:01:55.940
As you see here, this is a slide from Middlesex Hospital in London, by Julian Blum.

00:01:55.940 --> 00:02:05.340
He demonstrated that approximately 18 percent of patients with primary breast cancer survived after the,

00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:10.990
clinical onset of their disease at five years.

00:02:10.990 --> 00:02:19.950
This is not very good but we must accept this salvage rate and consider it in evaluating results of therapy.

00:02:19.950 --> 00:02:23.320
Can we have the next slide please.

00:02:23.320 --> 00:02:32.590
This next slide explains a paradox which all of us have been aware of, and had difficulty in explaining.

00:02:32.590 --> 00:02:40.530
This is that after a delay of a year or so, the salvage rate of breast cancer doesn't seem to be very much different

00:02:40.530 --> 00:02:47.390
from that in the group that is picked up early. The reason for this is shown in this slide.

00:02:47.390 --> 00:02:54.570
In the first year the grade one lesions survive very nicely,

00:02:54.570 --> 00:02:57.530
but the high grade lesions, the more aggressive lesions,

00:02:57.530 --> 00:03:00.550
die off rapidly, so at the end of the year,

00:03:00.550 --> 00:03:05.460
we have an entirely different set-up in that the more aggressive tumors

00:03:05.460 --> 00:03:11.100
have been weeded out of our material.

00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:18.220
The low-grade tumors certainly survive longer and all of them would survive longer with therapy, obviously.

00:03:18.220 --> 00:03:21.860
Can we have the next slide please.

00:03:21.860 --> 00:03:26.770
In the last sixty years, the salvage rate of breast cancer has improved,

00:03:26.770 --> 00:03:30.070
probably two- or three-fold, and this is due mainly to 

00:03:30.070 --> 00:03:33.320
the fact that we are getting patients at an earlier stage

00:03:33.320 --> 00:03:37.080
when the disease is more confined to the breast, and less likely

00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:41.950
to have spread beyond the confines of the breast and the regional nodes.

00:03:41.950 --> 00:03:50.730
This slide demonstrates the salvage rate obtained in a representative group of patients by radical mastectomy supplemented by

00:03:50.730 --> 00:03:58.180
x-ray therapy. As you see, when the disease is confined to the breast, salvage rate is very satisfactory. 

00:03:58.180 --> 00:04:05.200
As the nodes are involved from the lowermost level of the axilla to the upper level, the salvage drops.

00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:11.260
since there is more possibility or probability of systemic spread

00:04:11.340 --> 00:04:15.640
as the local extension of disease progresses. 

00:04:15.640 --> 00:04:22.660
This leads us to probably the most important factor in increasing our salvage rate at present,

00:04:22.660 --> 00:04:24.860
and this is early diagnosis. 

00:04:24.860 --> 00:04:28.240
Next slide please.

00:04:28.340 --> 00:04:36.780
One of the means of detecting the early patient is to entertain a constant suspicion

00:04:36.780 --> 00:04:39.950
of any persistent mass in the breast,

00:04:39.950 --> 00:04:43.570
whether it appears benign or malignant clinically.

00:04:43.570 --> 00:04:52.800
In our clinic at Memorial Hospital we found that roughly eleven percent of the patients who underwent local excision for either indefinite

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:58.400
or clinically benign disease proved to have carcinoma on frozen section.

00:04:58.400 --> 00:05:04.670
These patients did a lot better than the patients who came in with clinically obvious cancer of the breast. 

00:05:04.670 --> 00:05:12.430
Next slide please. To pursue this a bit further, we're not going to find the early lesions unless we look for them.

00:05:12.430 --> 00:05:22.670
Physical examination is absolutely essential; we can't depend solely on mammography or any other laboratory technique.

00:05:22.670 --> 00:05:29.560
The mere careful examination of the patient is most important. 

00:05:29.560 --> 00:05:36.700
This slide demonstrates a patient who on the right appears completely symmetrical.

00:05:36.700 --> 00:05:44.750
However, on elevating the arms, tensing the pectoral sheath, an obvious deformity in the left breast is apparent.

00:05:44.750 --> 00:05:47.720
Next slide please.

00:05:47.720 --> 00:05:53.760
Now, we can't depend upon the age of the patient to help us to decide whether to do a biopsy or not.

00:05:53.760 --> 00:05:58.270
Here are two patients who are on the ward service at Memorial at the same time.

00:05:58.270 --> 00:06:00.010
One was eighty, the other eighteen.

00:06:00.010 --> 00:06:02.510
Both of them had cancer of the breast.

00:06:02.510 --> 00:06:04.610
Next please.

00:06:04.610 --> 00:06:09.470
The clinical appearance of a lesion can very often be misleading

00:06:09.470 --> 00:06:17.560
and this necessitates surgical biopsy for any persistent mass,

00:06:17.560 --> 00:06:20.850
whether it appears benign or malignant.

00:06:20.850 --> 00:06:26.000
This...we'll show you a few representative slides here, go over them quickly please,

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:29.360
and these are nipple lesions which should be easily diagnosed but actually

00:06:29.360 --> 00:06:31.920
are very difficult to diagnose.

00:06:31.920 --> 00:06:38.750
The first one is a patient with an intraductal papilloma coming through the duct and appearing on the surface of the nipple.

00:06:38.750 --> 00:06:40.130
Next please.

00:06:40.130 --> 00:06:45.330
The next which looks very similar, is a patient with early Paget's disease of the nipple.

00:06:45.330 --> 00:06:46.720
The next please.

00:06:46.720 --> 00:06:55.720
The next slide is a representative picture of eczema of the nipple with a typical weeping, crested lesion of the nipple and areola. 

00:06:55.720 --> 00:06:59.550
And the next slide which looks very similar,

00:06:59.550 --> 00:07:03.820
actually proved to be Paget's when biopsy was taken.

00:07:03.820 --> 00:07:06.200
So that even when we can see these lesions on the surface,

00:07:06.200 --> 00:07:11.320
we really cannot have any confidence in our clinical ability to diagnose them accurately.

00:07:11.320 --> 00:07:13.420
Next please.

00:07:13.420 --> 00:07:19.280
Not only is it difficult to diagnose from clinical examination,

00:07:19.280 --> 00:07:24.740
we also have difficulty even when we have the specimen apparent in the operating room.

00:07:24.740 --> 00:07:28.410
This lesion, which appears rather mean,

00:07:28.410 --> 00:07:34.680
proved to be a benign intraductal pathol mitosis tissue in the breast. 

00:07:34.680 --> 00:07:40.920
And the next slide which looks very similar is a gelatinous carcinoma.

00:07:40.920 --> 00:07:45.570
So by all means forget any pride in your ability

00:07:45.570 --> 00:07:49.740
to make a definite clinical diagnosis of a breast lesion.

00:07:49.740 --> 00:07:53.500
If a mass persists in a breast, persists through a period,

00:07:53.500 --> 00:07:59.230
take it out to prove its characteristic and to rule out carcinoma.

00:07:59.230 --> 00:08:01.180
Next slide please.

00:08:01.180 --> 00:08:06.380
Now one of the best illustrations of the importance of early diagnosis 

00:08:06.380 --> 00:08:10.500
comes from clinical data supplied by cancer detection clinics.

00:08:10.500 --> 00:08:16.120
In this chart from the Minneapolis cancer detection clinic as well as from

00:08:16.120 --> 00:08:22.140
the Strang Clinic in New York, we see that if you look at the bottom two lines,

00:08:22.150 --> 00:08:25.780
the salvage rate of patients who are asymptomatic

00:08:25.780 --> 00:08:31.020
and in whom carcinoma of the breast was detected on routine examination,

00:08:31.020 --> 00:08:33.610
and the patient is then subjected to surgery,

00:08:33.610 --> 00:08:39.780
salvage rate of five years was 85 percent; the nodal involvement was only 30 percent.

00:08:39.780 --> 00:08:46.580
This is what we can expect to obtain in general practice if all individuals concerned

00:08:46.580 --> 00:08:51.380
would all cooperate: the patient, local physician, surgeon, radiologist.

00:08:51.380 --> 00:08:53.460
Next slide please.

00:08:53.460 --> 00:08:56.650
Now even though we would like to get the early patients,

00:08:56.650 --> 00:09:03.240
I don't think we can be too choosy when we have a situation like this. 

00:09:03.240 --> 00:09:06.680
Here's a patient who many would consider inoperable,

00:09:06.680 --> 00:09:10.600
with an ulcerated tumor of the breast, edema of the skin of the breast,

00:09:10.600 --> 00:09:12.340
large nodes in the axilla.

00:09:12.340 --> 00:09:16.160
However, we were unable to find any evidence of systemic disease,

00:09:16.160 --> 00:09:20.730
neck was clear, and we decided to do a radical mastectomy on this patient. 

00:09:20.730 --> 00:09:22.040
Next please.

00:09:22.040 --> 00:09:26.970
She proved to have an medullary carcinoma, all the nodes were negative, they're inflammatory. 

00:09:26.970 --> 00:09:30.390
The edema was caused by obstruction of the drainage 

00:09:30.390 --> 00:09:32.760
by mechanical pressure of the tumor

00:09:32.760 --> 00:09:36.250
and she is now fifteen years post-op free of disease.

00:09:36.250 --> 00:09:40.480
So I don't think that we do the best we can with our patients

00:09:40.480 --> 00:09:46.400
by being over-selective in considering operability.

00:09:46.400 --> 00:09:49.010
May I have the next slide please?

00:09:49.010 --> 00:09:54.730
Now in considering the technique of primary therapy,

00:09:54.730 --> 00:09:58.530
we'll devote most of our discussion to surgery, surgical therapy.

00:09:58.530 --> 00:10:01.980
This is our anatomical set-up.

00:10:01.980 --> 00:10:10.760
We can attack the breast tumor, its regional lymph node metastases, and the breast itself which,

00:10:10.760 --> 00:10:14.000
from which the tumor arises.

00:10:14.000 --> 00:10:17.810
Unfortunately we still do not have a good technique

00:10:17.810 --> 00:10:23.090
for dealing with systemic blood-borne metastasis.

00:10:23.090 --> 00:10:26.680
This again emphasizes the importance of early diagnosis.

00:10:26.680 --> 00:10:29.220
Now here is our anatomical set-up...

00:10:29.220 --> 00:10:33.660
The lymphatics of the breast drain to the axilla

00:10:33.660 --> 00:10:35.820
and to the internal mammary areas.

00:10:35.820 --> 00:10:39.530
Approximately three-quarters of the lymphatic drainage goes to the axilla

00:10:39.530 --> 00:10:43.210
and about one-quarter to the internal mammary chain.

00:10:43.210 --> 00:10:47.030
From either of these depots, further drainage extends into the base of the neck

00:10:47.030 --> 00:10:52.400
where the lymphatics drain into the large vessels behind the head of the clavicle.

00:10:52.400 --> 00:11:00.020
Now, there are roughly three categories of patients with cancer of the breast

00:11:00.020 --> 00:11:04.120
to be considered for the various surgical attacks.

00:11:04.120 --> 00:11:10.330
Number one would be the in-situ, or intraductal non-infiltrating cancers which are confined to the breast

00:11:10.330 --> 00:11:16.530
and in which there is almost no risk of regional node involvement.

00:11:16.530 --> 00:11:20.340
These patients do perfectly well with a modified radical.

00:11:20.340 --> 00:11:24.100
Essentially this consists of a complete simple mastectomy

00:11:24.100 --> 00:11:26.380
plus a low axillary dissection.

00:11:26.400 --> 00:11:32.160
Now, the great majority of patients are patients with early infiltrating breast cancers

00:11:32.160 --> 00:11:35.510
which arise in the upper outer portion of the breast.

00:11:35.510 --> 00:11:40.060
In these patients, the main risk of further spread is to the axilla,

00:11:40.060 --> 00:11:45.730
and these patients we believe should be treated, ideally, by radical mastectomy.

00:11:45.730 --> 00:11:52.060
Finally, we have a small group of patients in whom there is a high risk of internal mammary spread.

00:11:52.060 --> 00:11:56.260
Primarily those with lesions arising in the center and medial portion

00:11:56.260 --> 00:12:01.190
of the breast, and we personally prefer to do an extended radical on these patients,

00:12:01.190 --> 00:12:04.630
and treat the internal mammary area just as we do 

00:12:04.630 --> 00:12:08.590
the axilla in performing a radical mastectomy.

00:12:08.590 --> 00:12:11.330
Next slide please.

00:12:11.400 --> 00:12:16.960
Now this represents a patient with a very early Paget's of the nipple.

00:12:16.970 --> 00:12:19.140
With a set-up like this,

00:12:19.140 --> 00:12:24.320
particularly when no induration is palpable beneath the nipple in the duct system,

00:12:24.320 --> 00:12:26.630
the great likelihood is that we are dealing

00:12:26.630 --> 00:12:28.650
with early Paget's in the nipple

00:12:28.650 --> 00:12:35.110
and a non-infiltrating microscopic intraductal carcinoma in the underlying duct system.

00:12:35.110 --> 00:12:40.090
These patients almost never have axillary node metastasis

00:12:40.090 --> 00:12:43.650
and these patients do perfectly well, in our experience,

00:12:43.650 --> 00:12:47.360
when they are treated by the modified approach.

00:12:47.360 --> 00:12:49.130
Next please.

00:12:49.130 --> 00:12:56.170
The big advantage of doing a simple with a low axillary dissection on these optimal patients,

00:12:56.170 --> 00:12:59.410
with practically no risk of axillary metastasis,

00:12:59.410 --> 00:13:02.610
is the physical appearance following the surgery.

00:13:02.610 --> 00:13:06.180
This patient still has the pectoral muscles

00:13:06.180 --> 00:13:13.140
and obviously has a better physical appearance and slightly improved functional result

00:13:13.140 --> 00:13:16.840
than would occur following radical mastectomy.

00:13:16.840 --> 00:13:18.890
Next please.

00:13:18.890 --> 00:13:25.600
To prove that this is not an an inadequate therapy for these optimal lesions,

00:13:25.600 --> 00:13:30.530
we now have some 75 patients who underwent this procedure, 

00:13:30.530 --> 00:13:35.410
again for very early lesions, all of whom are free of disease,

00:13:35.410 --> 00:13:37.220
many over five years. 

00:13:37.220 --> 00:13:45.200
Now  this procedure is primarily indicated for the in-situ, non-infiltrating cancers of the breast,

00:13:45.200 --> 00:13:48.430
particularly when they are microscopic in size.

00:13:48.430 --> 00:13:54.770
With a bulky tumor, there is a chance that you might miss the infiltrating area in the breast

00:13:54.770 --> 00:13:59.550
and on this basis, this patient probably should have a radical mastectomy.

00:13:59.550 --> 00:14:04.040
If you look over the slide, you see that all these lesions are lesions 

00:14:04.040 --> 00:14:09.100
where we ordinarily don't find nodal involvement.

00:14:09.100 --> 00:14:11.450
Now so much for the modified approach.

00:14:11.450 --> 00:14:15.040
These patients do very well, and again,

00:14:15.040 --> 00:14:19.230
these patients are only found when all means at,

00:14:19.230 --> 00:14:24.680
utilizing all measures for early diagnosis are utilized.

00:14:24.680 --> 00:14:29.390
Mammography, cancer detection clinics, self-examination, and so forth.

00:14:29.390 --> 00:14:31.630
Next slide please.

00:14:31.630 --> 00:14:36.770
Now, the great majority of patients would be treated, in our experience,

00:14:36.770 --> 00:14:40.320
or should be treated in our experience, by a radical mastectomy.

00:14:40.320 --> 00:14:48.530
And this patient demonstrates the result of an adequate radical operation with thin flaps,

00:14:48.530 --> 00:14:56.620
complete cleaning out of the axillary content of the neurovascular bundle apparent beneath the skin in the axilla.

00:14:56.620 --> 00:14:58.390
Next please.

00:14:58.390 --> 00:15:02.200
Only through such radical surgery will we get good results.

00:15:02.200 --> 00:15:05.200
Unfortunately very often, or too often,

00:15:05.200 --> 00:15:09.530
we see a patient with a lesion, with an operation, of this sort  

00:15:09.530 --> 00:15:12.390
referred into the clinic for post-operative x-ray therapy

00:15:12.390 --> 00:15:15.740
with a note saying that so-and-so has had a radical mastectomy,

00:15:15.740 --> 00:15:21.110
would you please radiate the breast, and the chest wall, and the axilla.

00:15:21.110 --> 00:15:25.890
Well, this patient has not had an adequate radical, she hasn't even had a complete simple

00:15:25.890 --> 00:15:29.260
and this sort of surgery is to be condemned.

00:15:29.260 --> 00:15:35.620
Unless one depends completely on x-ray therapy, there's no place for such surgery in treating breast cancer.

00:15:35.620 --> 00:15:38.640
Next please.

00:15:38.640 --> 00:15:43.790
This is a representive slide showing the sort of salvage rate we can expect

00:15:43.790 --> 00:15:50.200
with the use of radical mastectomy in the average group of patients.

00:15:50.200 --> 00:15:56.130
These thousand patients were treated at Memorial Hospital between 1945 and '48, 

00:15:56.130 --> 00:15:58.620
and as you see the overall group, 

00:15:58.620 --> 00:16:03.550
50 percent were free of disease, 57 percent alive at five years.

00:16:03.550 --> 00:16:09.010
Now in the third box down, this is the group which was picked up

00:16:09.010 --> 00:16:12.850
by local excision of a clinically indefinite

00:16:12.850 --> 00:16:16.560
or benign lesion, and in this group we, 

00:16:16.560 --> 00:16:19.990
our salvage rate is almost 70 percent free of disease

00:16:19.990 --> 00:16:22.910
and close to 75 percent alive at five years.

00:16:22.910 --> 00:16:27.860
This is actually what we should obtain if all of us were on the ball,

00:16:27.860 --> 00:16:30.800
the patient included.

00:16:30.800 --> 00:16:34.540
Unfortunately, many patients included in this series were quite advanced

00:16:34.540 --> 00:16:40.530
and this is what brings down the survival rate in the overall group.

00:16:40.530 --> 00:16:45.050
We did not include our modified radicals in this evaluation.

00:16:45.050 --> 00:16:46.630
They're in the bottom box.

00:16:46.630 --> 00:16:51.780
As you see they are all free of disease, all in uninfiltrating lesions.

00:16:51.780 --> 00:16:54.280
Next please.

00:16:54.380 --> 00:17:00.540
Now to get to our third surgical effort, the extended radical.

00:17:00.540 --> 00:17:06.280
We would apply this to patients who have a high risk of internal mammary spread,

00:17:06.280 --> 00:17:11.320
and we essentially would remove the breast, the axillary content, the internal mammary chain, 

00:17:11.320 --> 00:17:18.050
all in continuity, and attempt to do an en bloc excision of this entire area.

00:17:18.050 --> 00:17:20.610
Next please.

00:17:20.610 --> 00:17:26.170
This slide demonstrates the, a plotting of the location of the internal mammary nodes

00:17:26.170 --> 00:17:31.530
in a series of patients explored by Darl Eveson and his group in Copenhagen

00:17:31.530 --> 00:17:35.520
and as you see, the nodes lie pretty much

00:17:35.520 --> 00:17:41.640
between the undersurface of the ipsilateral margin of the sternum and the costochondral junction.

00:17:41.640 --> 00:17:46.750
The dotted line is the area which we resect in removing this area. 

00:17:46.750 --> 00:17:49.270
Next please.

00:17:49.270 --> 00:17:54.210
We did some baseline studies in an effort to decide

00:17:54.210 --> 00:17:59.290
which patients would be most suitable for this extended approach.

00:17:59.290 --> 00:18:04.210
This patient demonstrates a parasternal recurrence in the second inner space

00:18:04.210 --> 00:18:10.180
there is a mound of cancer coming out of the chest in the intercostal space next to the sternum.

00:18:10.180 --> 00:18:16.420
We believe this represents an outgrowth of an internal mammary node metastasis,

00:18:16.420 --> 00:18:21.790
and we use this as an indirect index of frequency of internal mammary spread.

00:18:21.790 --> 00:18:24.030
Next please.

00:18:24.030 --> 00:18:29.450
On this slide we broke down a study of a thousand patients

00:18:29.450 --> 00:18:32.010
into the various quadrants of the breast

00:18:32.010 --> 00:18:35.830
and evaluated the incidence of parasternal recurrence

00:18:35.830 --> 00:18:38.760
as the first sign of recurrent cancer.

00:18:38.760 --> 00:18:43.990
In the overall group, five percent of the patients showed a parasternal recurrence

00:18:43.990 --> 00:18:46.530
as a first sign of recurrent cancer. 

00:18:46.530 --> 00:18:53.120
This material is based on data on patients treated between 1945 and '48

00:18:53.120 --> 00:18:57.320
before the advent of [?] therapy.

00:18:57.320 --> 00:19:00.070
The sectors next to the sternum, A and C,

00:19:00.070 --> 00:19:04.650
showed a particularly high incidence, 17 and 20 percent,

00:19:04.650 --> 00:19:08.560
Whereas the outer quadrants, F and G, showed only two percent

00:19:08.560 --> 00:19:11.650
incidence of parasternal recurrence.

00:19:11.650 --> 00:19:12.990
Next slide please.

00:19:12.990 --> 00:19:17.690
Again demonstrates the five year salvage in the same group of patients,

00:19:17.690 --> 00:19:22.200
and again we have a better salvage rate for the outer-quadrant lesions 

00:19:22.200 --> 00:19:24.970
than for the parasternal lesions.

00:19:24.970 --> 00:19:28.430
The worst salvage rate when the axillary nodes were negative

00:19:28.430 --> 00:19:31.930
was for sector A, in the extreme upper inner portion of the breast. 

00:19:31.930 --> 00:19:37.810
Only 54 percent of patients with negative axilla survived at five years. 

00:19:37.810 --> 00:19:41.120
Sector E is interesting, the subareola area, 

00:19:41.120 --> 00:19:45.200
in that these patients did very well when the axilla was negative

00:19:45.200 --> 00:19:48.240
and they did very poorly when the axilla was involved.

00:19:48.240 --> 00:19:50.730
Eighty-four percent salvage with a negative axilla,

00:19:50.730 --> 00:19:53.730
only 19 percent with a positive axilla

00:19:53.730 --> 00:19:59.440
And we have found that, by and large, with an infiltrating lesion beneath the areola,

00:19:59.440 --> 00:20:04.360
if the axilla is involved, about 60 percent have internal mammary involvement.

00:20:04.360 --> 00:20:08.750
When the axilla is negative, we almost never find anything in the internal mammary.

00:20:08.750 --> 00:20:14.360
So we have a baseline to compare results to the extended radical.

00:20:14.360 --> 00:20:17.610
And from studying this material we decided to apply the

00:20:17.610 --> 00:20:20.760
extended radical mastectomy mainly to patients

00:20:20.760 --> 00:20:24.570
whose lesions arose in sectors A, B, C, D, and E. 

00:20:24.570 --> 00:20:28.590
Very few in the outer quadrants. Next please.

00:20:28.590 --> 00:20:31.940
This is the technique that we use.

00:20:31.940 --> 00:20:40.200
Here you see, we perform our skin excision just as one would do this for a radical mastectomy.

00:20:40.200 --> 00:20:44.780
We go at least four cm from the nearest margin of the tumor.

00:20:44.780 --> 00:20:49.530
We keep our dissection in the flaps outside of the superficial fascia

00:20:49.530 --> 00:20:52.020
which separates the breast parenchyma

00:20:52.020 --> 00:20:54.280
from the subcutaneous fat,

00:20:54.280 --> 00:20:57.490
and we develop our flaps to the clavicle above the costal margin,

00:20:57.490 --> 00:21:01.260
below the sternum medially, and latissimus muscle laterally.

00:21:01.260 --> 00:21:04.050
Next please.

00:21:04.050 --> 00:21:06.070
In the inferior portion of the breast,

00:21:06.070 --> 00:21:11.440
we preserve the rectus sheath from the level of the sixth inner space inferiorly

00:21:11.440 --> 00:21:14.870
in order to avoid a diastasis recti.

00:21:14.870 --> 00:21:18.290
There is very, there is really no proof that the rectus sheath

00:21:18.290 --> 00:21:24.970
acts as a lymphatic spread to the abdomen and the liver particularly.

00:21:24.970 --> 00:21:29.370
We expose the muscle over the sixth rib,

00:21:29.370 --> 00:21:33.500
and cut through the rectus muscle over the sixth, over the fifth inner space.

00:21:33.500 --> 00:21:34.960
Next please.

00:21:34.960 --> 00:21:37.060
In the upper margin of the wound,

00:21:37.060 --> 00:21:40.160
we split the pectoralis major muscle

00:21:40.160 --> 00:21:45.010
between its two normal heads, the clavicular and the sternal head,

00:21:45.010 --> 00:21:47.480
through the normal plane. 

00:21:47.480 --> 00:21:52.970
This is found most easily beneath the head of the clavicle, medially. 

00:21:52.970 --> 00:22:01.280
After finding this area, dissect the two muscle bundles through the anatomical separation.

00:22:01.280 --> 00:22:06.700
We then transect the tendon of the muscle from its attachment on the humerus.

00:22:06.700 --> 00:22:08.970
Next please.

00:22:08.970 --> 00:22:14.260
Here, we now cut through the clavipectoral sheath

00:22:14.260 --> 00:22:19.060
which is attached to the coracobrachialis muscle laterally,

00:22:19.060 --> 00:22:23.440
cut through the tendon of the minor muscle, which is enveloped by the sheath,

00:22:23.440 --> 00:22:25.550
and its attachment to the coracoid,

00:22:25.550 --> 00:22:31.090
and cut it from its attachment to the subclavian muscle medially.

00:22:31.090 --> 00:22:38.120
This is then reflected downward and gives you a very nice approach to the axillary field.

00:22:38.120 --> 00:22:39.950
Next please.

00:22:39.950 --> 00:22:43.700
In performing, performing the extended radical,

00:22:43.700 --> 00:22:48.510
after developing our fields and isolating the operative area,

00:22:48.510 --> 00:22:53.800
we open the first inner space by exposing this area.

00:22:53.800 --> 00:23:00.860
by reflecting the sternal portion of the pectoralis major muscle downward.

00:23:00.860 --> 00:23:05.150
This exposes the first inner space

00:23:05.150 --> 00:23:11.210
just beneath the head of the manubrium, the arch of manubrium and the first rib.

00:23:11.210 --> 00:23:14.020
Next slide please.

00:23:14.020 --> 00:23:21.820
The pectoralis muscle is isolated by running a finger

00:23:21.820 --> 00:23:26.320
beneath the muscle where it is reflected over the chest wall

00:23:26.320 --> 00:23:29.740
lateral to its parasternal attachment.

00:23:29.740 --> 00:23:35.880
The finger above goes through the first inner space level down below...

00:23:35.880 --> 00:23:41.890
traverses the fifth, fourth, and third inner space levels.

00:23:41.890 --> 00:23:46.540
And this isolates and mobilizes the muscle,

00:23:46.540 --> 00:23:54.990
and delineates the parasternal attachment, which corresponds roughly to the costal cartilages.

00:23:54.990 --> 00:23:57.490
Next please.

00:23:57.490 --> 00:24:03.630
Now the segment of chest wall which was isolated in that manner,

00:24:03.630 --> 00:24:06.820
is now resected from the chest wall

00:24:06.820 --> 00:24:10.760
by cutting through the first inner space just beneath the first rib 

00:24:10.760 --> 00:24:12.780
and the arch of the manubrium, 

00:24:12.780 --> 00:24:14.710
transecting the internal mammary vessels

00:24:14.710 --> 00:24:16.380
beneath the arch of the manubrium,

00:24:16.380 --> 00:24:19.710
cutting through the parietal pleura at this level. 

00:24:19.710 --> 00:24:27.250
Doing the same thing inferiorly, cutting through the two branches of the internal mammary vessels, 

00:24:27.250 --> 00:24:36.050
just above the sixth rib, then splitting the sternum with a lebsche knife, splitting it approximately one-third in from its ipsilateral margin. 

00:24:36.050 --> 00:24:38.800
This creates a trap door in the chest wall.

00:24:38.800 --> 00:24:45.020
Then, we use a heavy shears and cut through the ribs and intercostal soft parts,

00:24:45.020 --> 00:24:48.960
just at the level of the costochondral junctions.

00:24:48.960 --> 00:24:50.500
Then we tuck this outward.

00:24:50.500 --> 00:24:55.110
There is still in continuity with the overlying pectoral muscles and breast.

00:24:55.110 --> 00:24:57.070
Next slide.

00:24:57.070 --> 00:25:01.050
Now the defect in the chest wall is repaired.

00:25:01.050 --> 00:25:06.530
First we suture the mediastinal pleura to the interior periosteum

00:25:06.530 --> 00:25:11.420
of the sternum to close off the mediastinum and act as a hemostatic measure. 

00:25:11.420 --> 00:25:18.460
Then we insert an underwater catheter through the fifth inner space laterally.

00:25:18.460 --> 00:25:22.520
Now the defect in the chest wall is stabilized

00:25:22.520 --> 00:25:26.910
and diminished in size through the use of stay sutures,

00:25:26.910 --> 00:25:34.630
which run from the cut surface of the sternum to the opposing rib margins.

00:25:34.630 --> 00:25:44.460
We usually put a, what corresponds to a vertical matrasuture, here and use heavy Dermalon sutures.

00:25:44.460 --> 00:25:49.470
These permanent stay sutures I, we believe 

00:25:49.470 --> 00:25:53.770
lessen the tendency toward loosening of this repair of the chest wall.

00:25:53.770 --> 00:25:56.640
Now, after stabilization of the chest wall defect,

00:25:56.640 --> 00:26:05.520
we then apply a graft of sterile ox fascia to the defect in the chest wall.

00:26:05.520 --> 00:26:08.340
This is tacked down with interrupted Dermalon,

00:26:08.340 --> 00:26:11.860
and then after the redundant margins are trimmed,

00:26:11.860 --> 00:26:18.790
it is smoothed down with a running, continuous locked, fine chromic catgut suture.

00:26:18.790 --> 00:26:23.110
In our experience, this material has proven quite satisfactory.

00:26:23.110 --> 00:26:27.810
It is much more convenient than using the patient's own fascia lata.

00:26:27.810 --> 00:26:35.130
Probably the fascia lata is a bit more durable and lasting.

00:26:35.130 --> 00:26:40.160
However, this seems to afford a practical means of closing the defect.

00:26:40.160 --> 00:26:42.590
Next please.

00:26:42.590 --> 00:26:44.300
Following closure of the chest wall,

00:26:44.300 --> 00:26:47.520
we now complete the radical mastectomy in the usual manner,

00:26:47.520 --> 00:26:49.950
usually preserving the long thoracic nerve

00:26:49.950 --> 00:26:52.430
and sacrificing the thoracodorsal.

00:26:52.430 --> 00:26:55.030
Next please.

00:26:55.030 --> 00:26:59.750
We ordinarily can obtain primary closure.

00:26:59.750 --> 00:27:03.040
If the flaps are somewhat tight we will, 

00:27:03.040 --> 00:27:07.570
we prefer to undermine the flaps across the midline, to mobilize them 

00:27:07.570 --> 00:27:11.280
rather than using a free graft.

00:27:11.280 --> 00:27:17.630
We use constant suction beneath the flaps, using a Hemovac or similar mechanism

00:27:17.630 --> 00:27:23.110
and have the underwater catheter come out through the lateral flap 

00:27:23.110 --> 00:27:27.560
and attach to an underwater bottle. 

00:27:27.560 --> 00:27:32.160
This catheter draining the chest cavity is removed two days post-operatively

00:27:32.160 --> 00:27:34.940
Next please.

00:27:34.940 --> 00:27:39.980
We ordinarily apply a wraparound dressing on the patient following this procedure,

00:27:39.980 --> 00:27:45.490
and this demonstrates a practical manner of holding the patient, supported on an arm board,

00:27:45.490 --> 00:27:47.980
while the dressing is wrapped about.

00:27:47.980 --> 00:27:50.620
Next please.

00:27:50.620 --> 00:27:58.430
This slide shows in a diagrammatic fashion the relationship between the tumor in the breast, 

00:27:58.430 --> 00:28:03.190
the lymphatic strain in this tumor, and the regional nodes in the axilla 

00:28:03.190 --> 00:28:06.050
as well as in the internal mammary areas. 

00:28:06.050 --> 00:28:11.050
We can do a true en bloc excision of this whole area by this means. 

00:28:11.050 --> 00:28:14.140
Next please.

00:28:14.140 --> 00:28:18.270
Here is a typical patient who was operated on fifteen years ago. 

00:28:18.270 --> 00:28:21.390
She had a lesion beneath the right areola.

00:28:21.390 --> 00:28:25.080
You can see some deviation of her nipple and a mark

00:28:25.080 --> 00:28:28.550
in the skin from the aspiration biopsy incision.

00:28:28.550 --> 00:28:30.780
Next please.

00:28:30.780 --> 00:28:33.290
This shows the specimen removed from this patient,

00:28:33.290 --> 00:28:38.750
demonstrating the tumor mass adjacent to the areola.

00:28:38.750 --> 00:28:42.140
The next slide shows the undersurface of the specimen

00:28:42.140 --> 00:28:45.520
which has already been cleared in the pathology laboratory.

00:28:45.520 --> 00:28:50.120
However, it gives us a good idea of the extent of chest wall excision. 

00:28:50.120 --> 00:28:54.990
You see the sternal margin, the second, third, fourth, and fifth costal cartilages. 

00:28:54.990 --> 00:28:57.020
Next please.

00:28:57.020 --> 00:29:03.120
Shows the patient with some added deformity in the depression next to the sternum. 

00:29:03.120 --> 00:29:05.910
This patient was one of our early patients

00:29:05.910 --> 00:29:10.710
At that time we inserted the fascia on the inside of the chest wall

00:29:10.710 --> 00:29:14.520
and obtained good support but added deformity.

00:29:14.520 --> 00:29:19.810
We now apply the fascia on the outside and avoid this depression next to the sternum.

00:29:19.810 --> 00:29:24.340
This patient had a positive node in the internal mammary area and a positive node in the axilla,

00:29:24.340 --> 00:29:27.250
and is now fifteen years free of disease.

00:29:27.250 --> 00:29:29.340
Next please.

00:29:29.340 --> 00:29:35.670
This slide shows the operative field following removal of the specimen

00:29:35.670 --> 00:29:39.780
and closure of the chest wall defect with a fascia lata graft.

00:29:39.780 --> 00:29:43.930
You see the axillary vessels and nerves up above, underwater catheter 

00:29:43.930 --> 00:29:50.240
draining the chest running down below thin flaps.

00:29:50.240 --> 00:29:52.500
Next please.

00:29:52.500 --> 00:29:55.060
This is the specimen removed from this patient,

00:29:55.060 --> 00:30:01.210
again demonstrating the in-continuity en bloc principle and showing the

00:30:01.210 --> 00:30:07.920
internal mammary vessels apparent beneath the parietal pleura of the chest resection.

00:30:07.920 --> 00:30:09.610
Next please.

00:30:09.610 --> 00:30:15.320
This is a patient, corresponding to the previous specimen,

00:30:15.320 --> 00:30:20.490
with very little added deformity as compared with a classical radical mastectomy.

00:30:20.490 --> 00:30:22.520
Next please.

00:30:22.520 --> 00:30:27.840
Here is another patient whose primary lesion arose in a second inner space next to the sternum,

00:30:27.840 --> 00:30:32.860
necessitating a rather unorthodox skin incision.

00:30:32.860 --> 00:30:38.090
We used the usual elliptical incision with a right-angle extension 

00:30:38.090 --> 00:30:41.750
going across the midline encompassing the tumor area

00:30:41.750 --> 00:30:45.470
and wound up with a T-type closure.

00:30:45.470 --> 00:30:49.500
Next please.

00:30:49.500 --> 00:30:54.640
We've now done over 600 patients at the Memorial Hospital using this technique.

00:30:54.640 --> 00:30:58.320
We've had two post-operative deaths within thirty days of surgery.

00:30:58.320 --> 00:31:02.310
One patient dying of a stroke and the other of a perforated peptic ulcer.

00:31:02.310 --> 00:31:07.040
This operative mortality is actually less than the operative mortality 

00:31:07.040 --> 00:31:09.740
of radical mastectomy in many clinics.

00:31:09.740 --> 00:31:14.820
It is only possible when very meticulous care is taken of patients, 

00:31:14.820 --> 00:31:19.290
and this procedure should be done only under ideal circumstances.

00:31:19.290 --> 00:31:22.390
Now because of our selection of patients,

00:31:22.390 --> 00:31:27.240
we have a relatively high incidence of internal mammary metastasis.

00:31:27.240 --> 00:31:32.230
As you see on the chart, although only 48 percent of our patients had axillary metastasis,

00:31:32.230 --> 00:31:35.320
33 percent had internal mammary disease.

00:31:35.320 --> 00:31:42.280
As a matter of fact, 15 percent of our patients with no metastasis in the axilla had internal mammary metastasis.

00:31:42.280 --> 00:31:49.810
If we had taken all comers our incidence of internal mammary spread would probably be somewhere around 12 percent only.

00:31:49.810 --> 00:31:52.190
Next please.

00:31:52.190 --> 00:31:56.300
It's all very well to take this out, but can you salvage these patients?

00:31:56.300 --> 00:32:00.150
Actually this slide demonstrates that you can salvage as many patients 

00:32:00.150 --> 00:32:04.930
who have internal mammary disease as you can patients with axillary disease.

00:32:04.930 --> 00:32:09.430
If you look at the third line, only axillary involvement, 

00:32:09.430 --> 00:32:13.600
you see that we have 57 percent of patients free of disease

00:32:13.600 --> 00:32:17.010
and 64 percent alive at five years.

00:32:17.010 --> 00:32:21.310
Go down two more lines and we have practically the same salvage rate 

00:32:21.310 --> 00:32:24.250
for patients with only internal mammary disease. 

00:32:24.250 --> 00:32:28.230
When both areas were involved, we still had a fairly respectable salvage rate: 

00:32:28.230 --> 00:32:32.560
45 percent alive and 37 percent free of disease.

00:32:32.560 --> 00:32:38.000
In the overall group, where 54 percent of the patients had positive nodes,

00:32:38.000 --> 00:32:41.150
we had 70 percent surviving at five years.

00:32:41.150 --> 00:32:47.490
Probably the most impressive figure is our low local recurrence rate, 7.3 percent.

00:32:47.490 --> 00:32:49.910
Next please.

00:32:49.910 --> 00:32:55.810
At ten years, we still have a fairly good salvage rate. 

00:32:55.810 --> 00:32:59.650
Ten year figures are quite impressive but they are difficult to evaluate

00:32:59.650 --> 00:33:04.450
because of the many incidental factors which occur over a long period of time

00:33:04.450 --> 00:33:06.970
to these patients.

00:33:06.970 --> 00:33:11.030
It is of interest in this group of 175 patients that 13 of them 

00:33:11.030 --> 00:33:13.940
developed a new cancer in the opposite breast.

00:33:13.940 --> 00:33:16.960
Also that 15 of these patients died of other disease

00:33:16.960 --> 00:33:19.010
without evidence of breast cancer. 

00:33:19.010 --> 00:33:21.600
They're all included in the figures.

00:33:21.600 --> 00:33:29.130
And as you see here, patients with internal mammary disease, we had 33 percent free of disease at ten years

00:33:29.130 --> 00:33:32.680
and 22 percent of patients with both axillary and internal mammary

00:33:32.680 --> 00:33:36.440
were still free of the disease at ten years.

00:33:36.440 --> 00:33:41.030
Despite the loss of patients to other diseases,

00:33:41.030 --> 00:33:45.050
49 percent of the original group were alive at five years,

00:33:45.050 --> 00:33:50.340
and 45 percent, at ten years, excuse me, and 45 percent free of disease. 

00:33:50.340 --> 00:33:52.560
Next please.

00:33:52.560 --> 00:33:57.600
Now, when considering salvage rates,

00:33:57.600 --> 00:34:03.000
I think the important thing is to consider overall salvage rate.

00:34:03.000 --> 00:34:05.880
Most statistics are based on selected series

00:34:05.880 --> 00:34:09.160
and we can select series that are particularly favorable.

00:34:09.160 --> 00:34:13.830
Or for some reason or another, select series which emphasize

00:34:13.830 --> 00:34:19.060
one of several factors involved in this problem.

00:34:19.060 --> 00:34:21.110
When we consider the overall approach,

00:34:21.110 --> 00:34:25.680
we find that the more adequate the primary therapy,

00:34:25.680 --> 00:34:28.010
the better the overall salvage rate.

00:34:28.010 --> 00:34:30.480
The importance of considering overall salvage rate is that

00:34:30.480 --> 00:34:33.640
it eliminates the bias of selection.

00:34:33.640 --> 00:34:38.350
Now in this slide here, we see a series of statistics

00:34:38.350 --> 00:34:43.520
covering various clinics treating breast cancer mainly by radical mastectomy,

00:34:43.520 --> 00:34:49.550
and comparing them with McWhirter's material based upon the simple mastectomy and x-ray therapy.

00:34:49.550 --> 00:34:54.170
And roughly, the radical mastectomy series is anywhere from seven to nine percent better

00:34:54.170 --> 00:34:56.820
than the simple mastectomy series.

00:34:56.820 --> 00:35:01.020
The more recent statistics certainly show improved results, 

00:35:01.020 --> 00:35:06.370
primarily because of better material involved in the series.

00:35:06.370 --> 00:35:09.170
Next please.

00:35:09.170 --> 00:35:12.970
The overall picture at the Memorial Clinic in New York City between 

00:35:12.970 --> 00:35:18.000
'53 and '55 showed that in the overall group, 

00:35:18.000 --> 00:35:22.970
we had over 54 percent alive at five years.

00:35:22.970 --> 00:35:26.700
This is despite a loss of two and a half percent to follow-up.

00:35:26.700 --> 00:35:31.450
In our primary operable group, which comprised 88 percent of the overall group,

00:35:31.450 --> 00:35:35.390
a five-year salvage was 80, 80, 60 percent.

00:35:35.390 --> 00:35:37.830
And in the patients we considered inoperable

00:35:37.830 --> 00:35:42.120
who comprised twelve percent of the overall group,

00:35:42.120 --> 00:35:46.960
ten percent were inoperable because of demonstration of systemic disease

00:35:46.960 --> 00:35:50.880
and only two percent because of extensive local disease.

00:35:50.880 --> 00:35:53.600
Next please.

00:35:53.600 --> 00:35:58.810
In private practice more recently, the salvage rate is certainly better.

00:35:58.810 --> 00:36:04.350
Again, mainly due to the fact that we have more favorable material.

00:36:04.350 --> 00:36:12.490
This is the overall picture of a series of patients treated between 1957 and '59,

00:36:12.490 --> 00:36:18.710
and as you see in this group, the majority of patients were treated by radical mastectomy,

00:36:18.710 --> 00:36:21.430
74 percent alive at five years.

00:36:21.430 --> 00:36:23.450
A good number treated by extended radical,

00:36:23.450 --> 00:36:25.320
75 percent alive.

00:36:25.320 --> 00:36:30.840
The total group, 74.3 percent salvage at five years.

00:36:30.840 --> 00:36:35.150
In the inoperable group, which comprised only six percent of this group,

00:36:35.150 --> 00:36:36.970
no patients were alive at five years.

00:36:36.970 --> 00:36:38.890
They really were inoperable.

00:36:38.890 --> 00:36:42.160
In the overall group we had 70 percent five-year salvage,

00:36:42.160 --> 00:36:49.290
and we believe this demonstrates the advantage of an aggressive approach,

00:36:49.290 --> 00:36:55.400
with liberal criteria of operability, and aggressive surgery. 

00:36:55.400 --> 00:37:00.990
Now to sum this all up, I think we could sum it up in this manner.

00:37:00.990 --> 00:37:04.790
That recent improvement in the salvage of primary breast cancer patients

00:37:04.790 --> 00:37:11.310
has been due to a combination of early diagnosis and continued aggressive surgical therapy,

00:37:11.310 --> 00:37:14.790
supplemented with x-ray therapy when indicated.

00:37:14.790 --> 00:37:19.000
Early diagnosis obtained through professional and public awareness

00:37:19.000 --> 00:37:24.440
of the need for early detection provides more patients with localized disease,

00:37:24.440 --> 00:37:28.340
and less risk of systemic disease at the time of treatment. 

00:37:28.340 --> 00:37:32.740
This great potential for improvement afforded by early diagnosis

00:37:32.740 --> 00:37:36.550
should not be canceled by inadequate primary therapy.

00:37:36.550 --> 00:37:40.800
At present we believe the main aim in the primary therapy of breast cancer

00:37:40.800 --> 00:37:45.690
will be to accomplish the complete eradication of the primary tumor 

00:37:45.690 --> 00:37:51.030
and its regional node metastasis by surgery and x-ray therapy.

